# Line endings are pinned, not left to each machine's core.autocrlf.
#
# This matters beyond tidiness: the launcher ships relay-routes.json and the two PowerShell helpers
# inside the jar and records a SHA-256 of each one in work/install-manifest.txt. If a checkout on
# Windows rewrote those files to CRLF while the released jar carried LF, the same release would hash
# differently on two machines, and the "has the user edited this file?" check would start answering
# wrongly - the failure mode being a silently overwritten route table.
#
# LF everywhere. PowerShell, javac and the JVM all read LF on Windows without complaint, and the
# pinned ending means the jar built from a Windows checkout is byte-identical to the one built in CI.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats that must never be touched.
*.jar   binary
*.jks   binary
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.zip   binary
*.dll   binary
*.exe   binary
